nal", "content": {"content_type": "text", "parts": ["Yep. Use this **same prompt in each of the three affected chats tonight**. Don’t let them fix anything yet; just make them reconstruct what actually happened so tomorrow we can compare the three failures side by side.\n\n```text\nGCE SESSION POSTMORTEM — LOOP / FRAME DRIFT RECON\n\nAUTHORITY: Don\nMODE: READ-ONLY POSTMORTEM\nPURPOSE: Reconstruct this session exactly as it occurred and identify where the operating frame broke down.\n\nDO NOT:\n- continue the build\n- fix anything\n- redesign architecture\n- create new slices\n- create new contracts\n- certify anything\n- modify files\n- modify Drive\n- rationalize the session after the fact\n\nThis is forensic reconstruction only.\n\nGROUNDING:\nGCE operates identity-first and seat-first.\n\nDon = final authority / orchestrator.\nCoda = strategic advisor / governance / framing.\nVittorio = technical challenge / warboard.\nChiave = architecture gate when architecture is actually required.\nNico = implementation / builder.\nLuce = verification / evidence check.\nSpecialty seats remain bounded to their actual roles.\n\nThe concern being investigated is NOT primarily model quality.\n\nWe are investigating whether the session entered a LOOP STATE because:\n1. the session lost or never established a clear operating frame,\n2. project/context bleed blurred separate functions,\n3. small uncertainties were converted into additional slices,\n4. verification became recursive certification,\n5. findings from Luce/Nico/other seats were repeatedly reframed through Coda without sufficient shared state,\n6. execution was debugged/re-architected mid-runtime instead of completing one coherent build pass,\n7. the session stopped producing visible runnable outcomes for Don,\n8. missing session header/footer correlated with loss of framework discipline.\n\nRECONSTRUCT THIS CHAT FROM THE FIRST REAL TASK REQUEST FORWARD.\n\nProduce:\n\nA. ORIGINAL ASK\nState exactly what Don originally wanted.\nSeparate the actual requested outcome from anything added later by the system/seats.\n\nB. EXPECTED SIZE OF WORK\nClassify the original ask as:\n- trivial adjustment\n- bounded maintenance\n- normal build\n- architecture change\n- genuinely complex build\n\nExplain why.\n\nC. ACTUAL WORK PERFORMED\nList only things that were genuinely built, changed, tested, verified, or produced.\nDo not count discussion, proposed architecture, prompts, handoffs, or speculative plans as completed work.\n\nD. LOOP ONSET\nIdentify the earliest point where the session stopped moving directly toward the requested outcome.\nQuote or summarize the decision that caused the branch.\n\nE. LOOP MECHANISM\nTrace the loop:\nDon → Coda → seat → verification → Coda → new slice/reframe → seat → verification, etc.\n\nIdentify every place where:\n- a minor issue became a new slice\n- a verifier became a design influence\n- Coda inferred missing architecture\n- a builder was interrupted before completing an end-to-end pass\n- the same question was effectively answered more than once\n- confidence decreased despite additional work\n- process/certification replaced visible progress\n\nF. ASSUMPTIONS / INVENTED STATE\nIdentify anything treated as true without sufficient evidence.\nMark each:\nVERIFIED\nSUPPORTED\nASSUMED\nUNKNOWN\n\nG. PROJECT / CONTEXT BLEED\nDetermine whether unrelated project context, historical framework material, prior handoffs, or seat doctrine influenced decisions that were not required by the immediate task.\n\nDo not assume contamination merely because context existed.\nShow the actual causal connection if one exists.\n\nH. SLICE AUDIT\nList every slice/pass/gate created during the session.\n\nFor each one state:\n- why it existed\n- what concrete artifact it produced\n- whether it was actually necessary\n- whether it could have been absorbed into one coherent end-to-end build\n\nI. VERIFICATION AUDIT\nReview Luce/verification behavior.\n\nSeparate:\n- legitimate defec